      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "3df1a962-28ed-5ee8-927b-1c62b88a956c",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $x^{2} + 2 y^{2}=9.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,-2).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=9.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,-2)$ can be found by computing $\u2207f(1,-2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x)\\,i+(4 y)\\,j \\\\ \u2207f(1,-2) & =(2 (1))\\,i+(4 (-2))\\,j \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(-8)\\,j \\\\  & =\u27e82,-8\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\u2207f(1,-2)=\u27e82,-8\u27e9$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=\u27e8{v}_{x},{v}_{y}\u27e9$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(1)}{2} & =\\frac{y-(-2)}{-8} \\\\ (-8)(x-(1)) & =(2)(y-(-2)) \\\\ (-4)(x-(1)) & =(1)(y-(-2)) \\\\ 4 - 4 x & =y + 2 \\\\ 4 x + y - 2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
