      "question": {
        ":question/id": "c34c8b85-1410-5a9f-8287-4a3353c69bd2",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}=22.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,3).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=22.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,3)$ can be found by computing $\u2207f(-1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(8 x)\\,i+(4 y)\\,j \\\\ \u2207f(-1,3) & =(8 (-1))\\,i+(4 (3))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(12)\\,j \\\\  & =\u27e8-8,12\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\u2207f(-1,3)=\u27e8-8,12\u27e9$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=\u27e8{v}_{x},{v}_{y}\u27e9$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{-8} & =\\frac{y-(3)}{12} \\\\ (12)(x-(-1)) & =(-8)(y-(3)) \\\\ (3)(x-(-1)) & =(-2)(y-(3)) \\\\ 3 x + 3 & =6 - 2 y \\\\ 3 x + 2 y - 3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
