        ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $3 x^{2} + y^{2}=36.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,-3).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If we set $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=36.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,-3)$ can be found by computing $\u2207f(3,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(3 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(3 x^{2} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ \u2207f(3,-3) & =(6 (3))\\,i+(2 (-3))\\,j \\\\  & =(18)\\,i+(-6)\\,j \\\\  & =\u27e818,-6\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\u2207f(3,-3)=\u27e818,-6\u27e9$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=\u27e8{v}_{x},{v}_{y}\u27e9$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(3)}{18} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-6} \\\\ (-6)(x-(3)) & =(18)(y-(-3)) \\\\ (-1)(x-(3)) & =(3)(y-(-3)) \\\\ 3 - x & =3 y + 9 \\\\ x + 3 y + 6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
