        "title": "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "3c33c871-3aa8-55d7-b3c4-b164edfa69d7",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,-1).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If we set $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ can be found by computing $\u2207f(1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x)\\,i+(8 y)\\,j \\\\ \u2207f(1,-1) & =(6 (1))\\,i+(8 (-1))\\,j \\\\  & =(6)\\,i+(-8)\\,j \\\\  & =\u27e86,-8\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\u2207f(1,-1)=\u27e86,-8\u27e9$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=\u27e8{v}_{x},{v}_{y}\u27e9$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(1)}{6} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-8} \\\\ (-8)(x-(1)) & =(6)(y-(-1)) \\\\ (-4)(x-(1)) & =(3)(y-(-1)) \\\\ 4 - 4 x & =3 y + 3 \\\\ 4 x + 3 y - 1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
