        ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $x^{2} + y^{2}=16.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-4,0).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "If we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=16.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-4,0)$ can be found by computing $\u2207f(-4,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x^{2} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x^{2} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ \u2207f(-4,0) & =(2 (-4))\\,i+(2 (0))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(0)\\,j \\\\  & =\u27e8-8,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\u2207f(-4,0)=\u27e8-8,0\u27e9$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-4,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=\u27e8{v}_{x},{v}_{y}\u27e9$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-(-4))(0)-(y-(0))(-8) & =0 \\\\ 8 y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
